**What it solves.** The Factory pattern centralises object creation so callers depend on an interface, not on concrete classes. Use it when the exact type to create depends on input or configuration and you want to add types without changing client code.

**A typical exercise.** Create different notification senders (Email, SMS, Push) from a type string. Steps:

1. Define the product interface.
2. Implement concrete products.
3. Add a factory that returns the right one.
4. Show client code using only the interface.

```java
interface Notifier { void send(String msg); }

class EmailNotifier implements Notifier {
    public void send(String m) { System.out.println("Email: " + m); }
}
class SmsNotifier implements Notifier {
    public void send(String m) { System.out.println("SMS: " + m); }
}

class NotifierFactory {
    static Notifier create(String type) {
        switch (type.toLowerCase()) {
            case "email": return new EmailNotifier();
            case "sms":   return new SmsNotifier();
            default: throw new IllegalArgumentException("Unknown type: " + type);
        }
    }
}

// client
Notifier n = NotifierFactory.create("sms");
n.send("Hello");
```

**Improving it.** The `switch` still needs editing for each new type, which weakens Open/Closed. Options: a registry `Map<String, Supplier<Notifier>>` where new types register themselves, or the **Factory Method** variant, where subclasses of a creator override `createNotifier()`. For families of related objects (for example, matching UI widgets per platform) use **Abstract Factory**.

**While coding live.** Talk through your design first, name classes clearly, validate unknown input, keep it compilable, and run a couple of test cases. Mention that the factory can inject dependencies, return cached singletons or pooled objects, and makes tests easier because you can substitute fakes.

**Mistakes to avoid:** putting `new` calls in the client, returning concrete types instead of the interface, and over-engineering with Abstract Factory when a simple factory is enough.
